# UI Component Rules

## Tailwind v4 (CSS-first config)
- There is NO tailwind.config.ts file. Theme customization is done via @theme in your CSS.
- CSS entry point uses @import "tailwindcss" — NOT @tailwind base/components/utilities.
- Use @tailwindcss/vite plugin in vite.config.ts — NOT the PostCSS plugin.
- Gradient utilities: use bg-linear-to-* (NOT bg-gradient-to-*, which was v3).
- Colors are OKLCH (shadcn v4 default), not HSL.

## shadcn/ui
- Style: new-york (the current default). Not the deprecated "default" style.
- Components are copied into src/components/ui/ — they are source files, not an npm dependency.
- Add components with: npx shadcn@latest add [component-name]
- All shadcn components use data-slot attributes for styling hooks.
- Use sonner for toasts (NOT the deprecated shadcn toast component).
- Available primitives to use: Button, Dialog, Sheet, Card, DropdownMenu, Tabs, Select, Input, Textarea, Badge, Separator, ScrollArea, Popover, Command, Tooltip.

## Design Direction (MVP scaffolding phase)
- MVP is functional scaffolding first, polish later. Use shadcn defaults. Do not spend time on custom themes, animations, or micro-interactions.
- Dark mode is the primary theme. Set up dark class on <html> and use dark: variants. But don't obsess over dark mode styling at scaffold phase.
- Desktop-first. Target 1280px+ viewport. Use responsive Tailwind classes (md:, lg:) even though mobile is post-MVP — don't hardcode widths.
- Minimal chrome. Clean, functional layouts. Think Notion or Linear, not Pro Tools.

## Component Patterns
- Functional components with named exports.
- Props interfaces defined inline or in the same file.
- Use React 19 ref-as-prop pattern, not forwardRef.
- Composition over configuration — prefer compound components (e.g., <Card><CardHeader>...) over prop-heavy monoliths.
- Loading states: use Skeleton components from shadcn for async content.
- Empty states: always handle the "no data" case with a clear message and action (e.g., "No ideas yet. Capture your first idea.").

## Drag and Drop (@dnd-kit)
- Use @dnd-kit/core and @dnd-kit/sortable for:
  - Reordering ideas within a section
  - Moving ideas between sections (drag from one section, drop on another)
  - Reordering sections within a song
  - Reordering songs within an album track listing
- Use DndContext at the Song Workspace level, with SortableContext per section.
- Drop zones should have visual feedback (highlight on hover).
- After a drop, update sortOrder values in Dexie.

## Rich Text Editor (Tiptap 3)
- Used for: Production Journal entries, Album Global Notes, Album Reference Material.
- Packages: @tiptap/react, @tiptap/starter-kit, @tiptap/extension-image, @tiptap/extension-placeholder.
- Image embeds: handle image upload/paste, store image blobs, insert as base64 data URLs or blob URLs.
- Keep the toolbar minimal: bold, italic, headings (H2, H3), image insert, link. No tables, no code blocks, no colors.
- Store editor content as HTML string in the database (songJournalEntries.content, albums.globalNotes, etc.).

## Navigation
- Use client-side routing (React Router or TanStack Router).
- Routes: / (Home/Pool), /song/:id (Song Workspace), /album/:id (Album View).
- Sidebar or top nav with: Home (Pool), Songs, Albums. Keep it minimal.
- Quick Capture is a modal/overlay accessible from anywhere via a floating button or keyboard shortcut.
